#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "a4eeb5c3-c1d5-5eb2-bb4e-03de915e0dab" :question/math-academy-id "e-8579" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle {x(t)}, {y(t)} \\rangle   = x(0)y(0) + x(1)y(1) + x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nGiven that $p(t) = 1 + t + t^2$ and $q(t) = kt - t^2$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)+p(-1)q(-1) \\\\  & =(1)(0)+(3)(k-1)+(1)(-k-1) \\\\  & =0+(3k-3)+(-k-1) \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d31046-ad1b-5de2-8d2b-19c2ce8b2a0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8580" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,1\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,1\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x(t) = kt^2+3t$ and $y(t) = t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{2}+3t)(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(k{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{4}}{4}+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{4} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "a9ae1458-cfaf-5a3d-a1c8-d7d539480fd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8581" :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf x$ and $\\mathbf y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot k)+1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =6k-2-4 \\\\  & =6k-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6k-6 & =0 \\\\ 6k & =6 \\\\ k & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "f084687c-bbe3-5506-b803-ae2c7e450a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8582" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\n- $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\n- $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle  =  \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\cos{t} \\sin2t \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-\\frac{1}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3})-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors are orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8232307-82a4-53de-bad1-9c5b618328ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308865} {:answer/id #uuid "3aedfa59-331f-5b35-b154-846f12ea70d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308866} {:answer/id #uuid "2e02c947-d75f-5244-ab5f-91bb20564642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308867} {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} {:answer/id #uuid "d330537c-aaaf-59b8-8c4a-6fb16adf3eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a46a93a5-b2c7-5738-ab86-46da85f50f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308868} :answer-field/id #uuid "78235b23-fddb-536a-83c9-bb59105b98b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccbba1a-31b9-5014-bee2-85dad03abf72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140263" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1\\cdot (-2))+4\\cdot (1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-4+8+4k \\\\  & =4+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4+4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-4 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} {:answer/id #uuid "d0080e9c-95d4-5ed3-af87-271cc9f3c19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132592} {:answer/id #uuid "09b8a0a9-e693-5862-90fd-17e1cab9583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132593} {:answer/id #uuid "2db798ed-de8d-5f4e-8199-0378fc05ffd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132594} {:answer/id #uuid "53611b71-8da8-53bd-9ff8-777848081934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c75e8ba2-3603-5ca2-aab9-2a87a9acfec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0843c47f-c932-5f31-8986-c111c47d29f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d41fa515-e181-5501-be57-d1516f0b8643" :question/math-academy-id "q-43979" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2c2cc5-066e-586b-913e-9616e3b08805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308900} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} {:answer/id #uuid "13f15a45-f942-5440-8896-310216bebc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "5b76f3cd-6843-5530-aceb-701171cf9ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308903} {:answer/id #uuid "d4566944-231f-5940-a100-61d5f7d0fd49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e3b27-cb43-5239-9c1d-de600b14c135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} :answer-field/id #uuid "9277c21b-3673-537b-9988-c4808290f327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02e28179-afce-5ef7-836d-53202828246c" :question/math-academy-id "q-44058" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc931a4-5e07-560a-aad0-fbfc9adcee19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133281} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e78270-342c-591f-8aa5-a003848a1b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133282} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b1c128-eb64-54ef-9817-0e5ae8092cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133283} {:answer/id #uuid "1e374c1e-9a40-518f-b98e-b1f50ebd1846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133284} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7591bb-6b7b-54ff-99f3-e94a88a029e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7591bb-6b7b-54ff-99f3-e94a88a029e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133285} :answer-field/id #uuid "242691fe-5a34-534c-bd10-15887c4f3d02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133280}] :question/id #uuid "09585884-a23f-5d81-a9d8-5d380fd1cbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97353" :question/problem "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 5x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0a03d53-6e3c-5d7e-b39b-ef4ddd32654f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308935} {:answer/id #uuid "6aceb5ac-2608-5a7f-b4ec-4a352dfcb762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308936} {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} {:answer/id #uuid "07b25c68-780b-5e2d-adac-9ffe36983d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308938} {:answer/id #uuid "90426f37-ed93-5189-87cc-830edda9ee48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01084dae-567d-5a25-ab59-50bf4cc3bca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308937} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b6a2b1-f5ee-532b-aadb-ffde37c868e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131bc7fb-6fc9-5e64-83f4-c7089c6d2d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-97355" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-4)y(-4)+x(2)y(2)+x(4)y(4).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k-2t$ and $q(t)=5-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-4)q(-4)+p(2)q(2)+p(4)q(4) \\\\  & =(k+8)(5-8)+(k-4)(5-2)+(k-8)(5-8) \\\\  & =(-3k-24)+(3k-12)+(-3k+24) \\\\  & =-3k-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -3k-12 & =0 \\\\ 3k & =-12 \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4d7335-3ff1-55ee-988f-ecf05889acab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308957} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bb1ca9-dd2a-5e8b-bbb0-473210b7a8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308958} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7789c0-1483-5818-b458-87586c50ba3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308959} {:answer/id #uuid "77723d8b-97e0-5ecf-a784-4013011ecd3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6fafbc-3524-5f64-b459-3698df8b35ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308956} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b75cfa-37eb-56b3-9b86-260ea837f3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "579248ee-641f-5ef7-a9f1-c830a24589fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97440" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67cd2901-db7d-5a1f-ae27-2ba0b38c96a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308970} {:answer/id #uuid "9c34072c-5988-5515-a7ce-d328e36106f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308971} {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a2d029-3159-577d-9e55-4e3451b7c4ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308973} {:answer/id #uuid "f64bcfd0-22b7-5d04-b678-faecdf9a7255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e587b8c6-83f9-5d79-b369-b7791276bbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308972} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6030c2d-9773-598e-b8be-79fa3d4c9985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6302273c-fd2b-591e-9cb8-0335b24472a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97445" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=1$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62a2f835-498f-5b9e-9051-89c946558e56" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab0db5a-5463-5c63-9f36-b1f43b5a48c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133305} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7b3bb-9bd5-5178-b7e8-a5f43f2879d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133306} {:answer/id #uuid "0938b586-0bc2-5b6d-89fb-5600409406bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133307} {:answer/id #uuid "17fe6c4c-efd0-515f-8425-1da3e6fa4edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133308} {:answer/id #uuid "72eca67c-b54b-5a37-9cfc-f3bde7962300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab0db5a-5463-5c63-9f36-b1f43b5a48c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d07abe0-c51c-58fb-bc68-8fc7c6635e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133304}] :question/id #uuid "3c169d7e-31f7-5721-948b-24c63fd291d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-97451" :question/problem "Let $V = C[0, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{2π}f(t)g(t)dt$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t) = \\cos (3t)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 0$\n2. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 2π$\n3. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\cos α\\cos β = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β))$.*"}]}
